# 5. Practical 3 — Inspect the Routing Table

## Command

```bash
ip route
```

## Observation

My Linux environment displayed a route similar to:

```text
default via 172.18.32.1 dev eth0
172.18.32.0/20 dev eth0
```

## Interpretation

The first line represents the default route:

```text
default via 172.18.32.1 dev eth0
```

This means that traffic that does not have a more specific route can be forwarded through:

```text
172.18.32.1
```

using:

```text
eth0
```

The second route identifies the directly connected network:

```text
172.18.32.0/20
```

## What I Learned

The routing table tells Linux where network traffic should be sent.

I learned that the default gateway is important when communicating with destinations outside the directly connected network.

